RISKS:
- Low: Tables that were missing from the database (transactions, reviews, etc.) were skipped per D-001.10-E. If those tables are created in a later phase, they will lack tenant_id column and RLS protection unless a future migration adds them.
- Low: The backfill assigned all NULL tenant_id rows to the demo tenant. If existing data should have belonged to a different tenant, this would be incorrect. However, the database started with all tenant_id NULL and only one tenant (default) existed, so demo tenant is appropriate for seed data.
- Low: RLS enforcement depends on middleware setting app.tenant_id per connection. If any connection fails to set this GUC, RLS may restrict access unintentionally. The existing middleware (tenantContext.js) was not modified and is assumed to be correct.

